# packaging/debian/control -- irc-serve, a federated modern-spec IRC server in C.
#
# `#` COMMENTS ARE LEGAL HERE, which was checked rather than assumed: deb822(5)
# says "Lines starting with '#', without any preceding whitespace are comment lines
# that are only permitted in source package control files (debian/control) ... These
# comment lines are ignored, even between two continuation lines. They do not end
# logical lines." So a comment may sit between a field and its continuation lines.
# Note that "without any preceding whitespace" -- an INDENTED `#` is not a comment,
# it is a continuation line, so none of the comments below are indented.
#
# WHAT IS AND IS NOT IN Build-Depends, because an over-declared build-dep is not a
# harmless act of caution: it makes the package uninstallable from any archive that
# has not mirrored it, so a build-dep that is not needed is a distribution problem
# created by a packaging file.
#
# debhelper-compat (= 13): the ONE place the debhelper compat level is declared.
# There is deliberately NO debian/compat file. debhelper deprecated compat < 7 in
# version 13, and a compat file next to a debhelper-compat build-dep is
# contradictory -- one of the two wins and which one is not something a reader
# should have to work out. scripts/check-packaging.py fails if both appear, or if
# neither does.
#
# cmake (>= 3.20): the version is not decoration. CMakeLists.txt's first line is
# `cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.20)`, and cmake below that fails with its own
# error naming a minimum, which is a worse failure than a build-dep that says so
# before the build starts.
#
# NO OPENSSL, because the package builds the zero-dependency configuration.
# CMakeLists.txt's WITH_TLS option defaults to OFF and the packaged build passes
# -DWITH_TLS=OFF explicitly (see packaging/debian/rules), so libssl-dev is not
# needed and libssl must NOT be a dependency. src/CMakeLists.txt's
# `find_package(OpenSSL 1.1.1 REQUIRED)` is inside the WITH_TLS branch and is
# therefore never reached by this build. THE CONSEQUENCE, STATED RATHER THAN HIDDEN:
# THIS PACKAGE HAS NO TRANSPORT ENCRYPTION. -DWITH_TLS=ON plus a libssl-dev
# build-dep produces a TLS-capable binary in about three lines of diff, but that
# wants a second binary package (irc-serve-tls), because a TLS build and a non-TLS
# build of the same source cannot share one Package: stanza whose Depends: differs.
# That is a release decision, not a packaging detail, and it is not made here.
Source: irc-serve
Section: net
Priority: optional
Maintainer: TheFirstIstari <euphonium.ashe.f@gmail.com>
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13), cmake (>= 3.20)
Standards-Version: 4.6.2
Rules-Requires-Root: no
Homepage: https://github.com/TheFirstIstari/irc-serve
Vcs-Browser: https://github.com/TheFirstIstari/irc-serve
Vcs-Git: https://github.com/TheFirstIstari/irc-serve.git

Package: irc-serve
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Description: Federated modern-spec IRC server in C
 Performance-first, minimal memory footprint, federated nodes,
 load-balanced, auto-scaling capable.
 .
 This build has no transport encryption: it is configured with -DWITH_TLS=OFF, so
 it has no third-party runtime dependency at all. See SECURITY.md for the
 transport-encryption layer, which is available in the source tree but is not
 compiled into this package.